  "knowledgePattern": "Read the full rational-function range from an output-parameter quadratic",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Treat an output as a parameter",
      "body": "<p>Name a possible output y and rearrange the function equation into a polynomial equation in x.</p>",
      "recap": "Renaming a quantity can turn a range problem into an algebraic equation, but admissibility must be kept.",
      "pitfall": "Check whether any parameter value changes the degree of your equation.",
      "pause": "Write the equation whose real solutions correspond to attainable outputs.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "Renaming a quantity can turn a range problem into an algebraic equation, but admissibility must be kept."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Require a real input",
      "body": "<p>Apply the discriminant condition where the equation is quadratic, and check the exceptional degree separately.</p>",
      "recap": "A nonnegative discriminant gives at least one real quadratic root; a vanished leading coefficient needs its own check.",
      "pitfall": "A boundary bound must be attainable before calling it a maximum or minimum.",
      "pause": "Find the full output interval and calculate the difference between its attained extremes.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "A nonnegative discriminant gives at least one real quadratic root; a vanished leading coefficient needs its own check."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The denominator is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real x. Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and rearrange to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, a real x exists exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. The missing case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is indeed attained at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The extreme values are attained: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Their difference is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, answer A.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> An independent bound check is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Both equality cases agree with the calculated extrema.</p>"
}
